T}
.TE
.sp
.sp
This behaviour is not what happens when PCRE2 is called via its POSIX
API. By default, PCRE2\(cqs behaviour is the same as Perl\(cqs, except that there is
no equivalent for \f(CBPCRE2_DOLLAR_ENDONLY\fP in Perl. In both PCRE2 and Perl, there
is no way to stop newline from matching \f(CI[^a]\fP.
.sp
Default POSIX newline handling can be obtained by setting \f(CBPCRE2_DOTALL\fP and
\f(CBPCRE2_DOLLAR_ENDONLY\fP when calling \f(CBpcre2_compile()\fP directly, but there is
no way to make PCRE2 behave exactly as for the \f(CBREG_NEWLINE\fP action. When using
the POSIX API, passing \f(CBREG_NEWLINE\fP to PCRE2\(cqs \f(CBpcre2_regcomp()\fP function
causes \f(CBPCRE2_MULTILINE\fP to be passed to \f(CBpcre2_compile()\fP, and \f(CBREG_DOTALL\fP
passes \f(CBPCRE2_DOTALL\fP. There is no way to pass \f(CBPCRE2_DOLLAR_ENDONLY\fP.
.SH "MATCHING A PATTERN"
.sp
The function \f(CBpcre2_regexec()\fP is called to match a compiled pattern
\f(CIpreg\fP against a given \f(CIstring\fP, which is by default terminated by a
zero byte (but see \f(CBREG_STARTEND\fP below), subject to the options in \f(CIeflags\fP.
Any option bits other than those listed below cause \f(CBREG_INVARG\fP to be
returned.
.sp
.RS 4
.PD
.TP 13
\f(CBREG_NOTBOL\fP
.ad l
The \f(CBPCRE2_NOTBOL\fP option is set when calling the underlying PCRE2 matching
function.
.TP 13
\f(CBREG_NOTEMPTY\fP
.ad l
The \f(CBPCRE2_NOTEMPTY\fP option is set when calling the underlying PCRE2 matching
function. Note that \f(CBREG_NOTEMPTY\fP is not part of the POSIX standard. However,
setting this option can give more POSIX\-like behaviour in some situations.
.TP 13
\f(CBREG_NOTEOL\fP
.ad l
The \f(CBPCRE2_NOTEOL\fP option is set when calling the underlying PCRE2 matching
function.
.TP 13
\f(CBREG_STARTEND\fP
.ad l
When this option is set, the subject string starts at \f(CIstring\fP\f(CR \fP\f(CR+\fP\f(CR \fP\f(CIpmatch\fP\f(CR[0].\fP\f(CIrm_so\fP
and ends at \f(CIstring\fP\f(CR \fP\f(CR+\fP\f(CR \fP\f(CIpmatch\fP\f(CR[0].\fP\f(CIrm_eo\fP, which
should point to the first character beyond the string. There may be binary
zeros within the subject string, and indeed, using \f(CBREG_STARTEND\fP is the only
way to pass a subject string that contains a binary zero.
.RS 13
.sp
Whatever the value of \f(CRpmatch[0].rm_so\fP, the offsets of the matched string
and any captured substrings are still given relative to the start of
\f(CIstring\fP itself. (Before PCRE2 release 10.30 these were given relative to
\f(CIstring\fP\f(CR \fP\f(CR+\fP\f(CR \fP\f(CIpmatch\fP\f(CR[0].\fP\f(CIrm_so\fP, but this differs from other
implementations.)
.sp
This is a BSD extension, compatible with but not specified by IEEE Standard
1003.2 (POSIX.2), and should be used with caution in software intended to be
portable to other systems. Note that a non\-zero \f(CIrm_so\fP does not imply
\f(CBREG_NOTBOL\fP; \f(CBREG_STARTEND\fP affects only the location and length of the string,
not how it is matched. Setting \f(CBREG_STARTEND\fP and passing \f(CIpmatch\fP as \f(CBNULL\fP
are mutually exclusive; the error \f(CBREG_INVARG\fP is returned.
.RE
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
If the pattern was compiled with the \f(CBREG_NOSUB\fP flag, no data about any matched
strings is returned. The \f(CInmatch\fP and \f(CIpmatch\fP arguments of
\f(CBpcre2_regexec()\fP are ignored (except possibly as input for \f(CBREG_STARTEND\fP).
.sp
The value of \f(CInmatch\fP may be zero, and the value \f(CIpmatch\fP may be \f(CBNULL\fP
(unless \f(CBREG_STARTEND\fP is set); in both these cases no data about any matched
strings is returned.
.sp
Otherwise, the portion of the string that was matched, and also any captured
substrings, are returned via the \f(CIpmatch\fP argument, which points to an
array of \f(CInmatch\fP structures of type \f(CRregmatch_t\fP, containing the
members \f(CIrm_so\fP and \f(CIrm_eo\fP. These contain the byte offset to the first
character of each substring and the offset to the first character after the end
of each substring, respectively. The 0th element of the vector relates to the
entire portion of \f(CIstring\fP that was matched; subsequent elements relate to
the capturing subpatterns of the regular expression. Unused entries in the
array have both structure members set to \-1.
.sp
\f(CRregmatch_t\fP as well as the \f(CRregoff_t\fP typedef it uses are defined in
\f(CRpcre2posix.h\fP and are not warranted to have the same size or layout as other
similarly named types from other libraries that provide POSIX\-style matching.
.sp
A successful match yields a zero return; various error codes are defined in the
header file, of which \f(CBREG_NOMATCH\fP is the "expected" failure code.
Memory allocation failures and heap, match, or depth limit exhaustion return
\f(CBREG_ESPACE\fP. Pattern errors detected during matching, such as recursion
loops or invalid uses of \f(CI\(rsK\fP, return \f(CBREG_BADPAT\fP.
.SH "ERROR MESSAGES"
.sp
The \f(CBpcre2_regerror()\fP function maps a non\-zero errorcode from either
\f(CBpcre2_regcomp()\fP or \f(CBpcre2_regexec()\fP to a printable message. If
\f(CIpreg\fP is not \f(CBNULL\fP, the error should have arisen from the use of that
structure. A message terminated by a binary zero is placed in \f(CIerrbuf\fP. If
the buffer is too short, only the first \f(CIerrbuf_size\fP \- 1 characters of the
error message are used. The yield of the function is the size of buffer needed
to hold the whole message, including the terminating zero. This value is
greater than \f(CIerrbuf_size\fP if the message was truncated.
.SH "MEMORY USAGE"
.sp
Compiling a regular expression causes memory to be allocated and associated
with the \f(CIpreg\fP structure. The function \f(CBpcre2_regfree()\fP frees all
such memory, after which \f(CIpreg\fP may no longer be used as a compiled
expression.
